WEBVTT

00:00:00.679 --> 00:00:19.999
<v Riley>Hey, welcome back. Nominations closed this week, so today's about who's actually on the ballot this October — Walt Cobb returning to Williams Lake with a landslide behind him, and a first-time challenger shaking up the South Cariboo race instead. Plus a water station delay worth watching. That and more, coming right up.

00:00:33.474 --> 00:00:52.802
<v Riley>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities — I'm Riley. It's Saturday, September 12th, 2026. Reaching you from the heart of the Cariboo, the homeland of the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh nations. Today's theme is Cariboo Local Affairs.

00:00:53.802 --> 00:01:02.098
<v Casey>And I'm Casey. Nominations for the Williams Lake election closed yesterday afternoon, so today's about who's actually asking for your vote this October.

00:01:02.198 --> 00:01:20.790
<v Casey>Quick weather check — clear skies out at Horsefly Lake this morning, around 6 degrees, with a high of 18 expected today. Across the region it's much the same story: 100 Mile House and Quesnel both sitting around 6, Williams Lake at 7, Hanceville at 7. Mainly clear right through the day, no rain in the forecast.

00:01:21.519 --> 00:01:25.999
<v Riley>Good day to be outside instead of arguing about council agendas. Let's get into it.

00:01:27.399 --> 00:01:39.391
<v Casey>Shorter list today than usual, but two of these stories deserve full attention, and the rest are worth a proper mention too. We'll start close to home, with two collisions that happened within a day of each other on Cariboo roads.

00:01:40.862 --> 00:02:04.494
<v Riley>One person was airlifted to hospital following a single-vehicle collision west of Williams Lake on Friday afternoon. A separate rollover happened the same week in Farwell Canyon, south of Riske Creek, where Central Cariboo Search and Rescue and BC Ambulance responded to a single-vehicle incident around seven in the morning. That person was also flown out, in critical condition.

00:02:05.955 --> 00:02:17.443
<v Casey>Two serious single-vehicle incidents on Cariboo highways in the same stretch of days. Farwell Canyon's road is notoriously steep and winding — locals know to slow down through there, visitors sometimes don't.

00:02:18.847 --> 00:02:29.991
<v Riley>From the highway to the courthouse. A Quesnel man, Harley William Lynn Soare, appeared in provincial court Thursday facing multiple charges after allegedly ramming police cars in the city.

00:02:30.668 --> 00:02:33.924
<v Casey>That's a serious charge sheet. Any detail on what led up to it?

00:02:35.382 --> 00:02:44.766
<v Riley>The Cariboo Observer's coverage is limited so far — court appearance details only, no timeline of the incident itself. We'll watch for the next court date.

00:02:45.766 --> 00:03:00.550
<v Casey>Ottawa has agreed to match donations raised by the Red Cross for Summerland wildfire relief. The Red Cross has been collecting funds for residents displaced by that fire, and the federal government's matching commitment effectively doubles whatever the public contributes.

00:03:01.887 --> 00:03:26.479
<v Riley>Summerland's an Okanagan story, not a Cariboo one, but the mechanism matters, because it's the same one this region leans on after every bad fire season. The Red Cross decides how the combined pool gets spent, which means donors are trusting an intermediary, not directing dollars themselves. Matching programs like this typically run on a deadline — if you're planning to give, sooner is safer than waiting.

00:03:27.479 --> 00:03:42.159
<v Casey>Provincial politics moved again this week too. Richmond-Bridgeport MLA Teresa Wat has joined the new party being built by Peter Milobar, the former BC Conservative who split off last month. That brings the still-unnamed party's total seats in the legislature to six.

00:03:43.342 --> 00:03:49.446
<v Riley>Six seats and no party name yet. That's an unusual place to sit six weeks before a fall session opens.

00:03:50.742 --> 00:04:06.494
<v Casey>It matters here because our own Cariboo MLAs sit inside these shifting caucus lines, and caucus math decides who chairs the committees that touch forestry policy and rural health funding. A party that grows from four seats to six mid-session changes who gets a seat at those tables.

00:04:07.494 --> 00:04:29.382
<v Riley>The province also announced new funding this week aimed at helping more communities dial 911 directly. Right now, a number of rural, remote, and Indigenous communities route emergency calls through longer chains — sometimes through a provincial dispatch centre, sometimes through a neighboring detachment, adding minutes that matter in an emergency.

00:04:30.760 --> 00:04:42.472
<v Casey>Every minute shaved off a 911 routing chain is measured in survival odds, not convenience. The release didn't specify which Cariboo communities qualify, so that's one to watch as the funding rolls out.

00:04:43.471 --> 00:05:00.159
<v Riley>Stepping outside the region for a moment — The Tyee ran a reflection this week on Wendell Berry, the American farmer and essayist who spent six decades arguing that rural places carry their own kind of intelligence, one that cities and universities routinely dismiss or ignore.

00:05:01.347 --> 00:05:18.771
<v Casey>Berry's whole body of work says a farm teaches a person things a spreadsheet never will — how weather actually behaves, how soil actually recovers, what a neighbor actually owes another neighbor. Feels relevant to a region that still measures a lot of its decisions in growing seasons and snowpack rather than fiscal quarters.

00:05:19.571 --> 00:05:37.851
<v Riley>On the science side, researchers have built a nanolaser small enough to sit on a microchip, using pulses of light instead of electrical signals to move information between components. ScienceDaily reports the approach could cut the energy computers use nearly in half, if it scales into commercial chips.

00:05:39.182 --> 00:06:04.518
<v Casey>Every data centre running the AI tools this show relies on would take that deal immediately. Energy costs are the single biggest constraint on how far these systems can scale. A separate team also built a bright white material without any titanium dioxide pigment or PFAS chemicals, taking design cues from how snow and clouds scatter light. Could show up eventually in paint, packaging, even textiles that don't leave forever-chemicals behind in local watersheds.

00:06:05.318 --> 00:06:24.334
<v Riley>The New York Times covered a social media influencer's decision to get tested for a BRCA gene mutation after her sister also tested positive, which reopened a broader conversation about who genetic testing actually makes sense for. It's not recommended for everyone — it depends heavily on family history.

00:06:25.741 --> 00:06:30.773
<v Casey>Not a Cariboo story specifically, but talk to your own doctor if it applies to your family.

00:06:32.008 --> 00:06:57.000
<v Riley>New Atlas has a piece on ports in the US and UK preparing for nuclear-powered cargo ships. Lloyd's Register, Maersk, and the ports of Charleston and Felixstowe have launched something called the Pink Corridor Project, mapping out the safety and regulatory groundwork needed before these ships start calling at commercial ports. Estimates run as high as fifty such vessels at sea by 2035.

00:06:58.119 --> 00:07:07.751
<v Casey>None of them are calling on Prince Rupert any time soon, but if this scales, it eventually reshapes shipping insurance and port infrastructure everywhere, including on this coast.

00:07:08.550 --> 00:07:43.438
<v Riley>One quick flag on US policy, since it crosses the border through grocery stores — a frozen blueberry recall tied to an E. coli outbreak has expanded to include product sold at Walmart, with hospitalizations rising. Not something Canadians vote on, but cross-border supply chains mean recalls here sometimes follow within days. The New Yorker also reviewed the new monster movie "Hope" this week, calling it violent small-town comedy colliding with big-budget Hollywood imperialism, and Atlas Obscura profiled a new monument in New York City honouring Green Berets and Special Forces soldiers from the post-9/11 era.

00:07:43.978 --> 00:07:46.506
<v Casey>Both fit if that's your kind of Saturday reading.

00:07:47.706 --> 00:08:07.914
<v Riley>We'll close where the tinkerers usually take us. Hackaday has a writeup on a homebrew Super Nintendo game built entirely in hand-coded assembly language — no modern development shortcuts, no emulator scaffolding, just someone writing machine instructions the same way the console's original engineers did back in the early nineties.

00:08:09.304 --> 00:08:32.368
<v Casey>That's the opposite of everything the AI coding tools we cover on this show promise. Somebody chose the hard way on purpose, cartridge and all. And it runs, on real hardware, using techniques that were already considered old-fashioned twenty years ago. The skill didn't vanish — it just went underground into a small community of people who kept doing it because they wanted to, not because anyone was paying them to.

00:08:33.831 --> 00:08:39.767
<v Riley>Not every skill needs an institution behind it to survive. Sometimes it just needs a handful of stubborn people.

00:08:41.163 --> 00:08:47.067
<v Casey>From homebrew assembly code to the people asking for your actual vote this fall. Let's turn to the spotlight.

00:08:48.466 --> 00:09:14.970
<v Riley>Today's spotlight is on the Quesnel and District Hospice Palliative Care Association. They provide free volunteer support to Quesnel and area residents facing a life-limiting illness — practical help around the home, grief support for families, and community education about dying well. No referral needed, no cost attached, just people showing up for neighbors during the hardest stretch of their lives. Find them at qdhpca.org.

00:09:15.497 --> 00:09:22.145
<v Casey>That's the kind of infrastructure that never shows up on a council agenda but holds a town together when everything else is falling apart.

00:09:22.745 --> 00:09:27.553
<v Riley>Speaking of what holds a town together — that's exactly where today's Deep Dive is headed.

00:09:28.953 --> 00:09:37.617
<v Casey>Williams Lake's mayoral race is set. Nominations closed Friday at four o'clock, and it's shaping up as a rematch of sorts — Walt Cobb has filed to run again.

00:09:38.200 --> 00:09:49.192
<v Riley>Cobb was mayor before this. Last time he ran, he won with 60.4 percent of the vote — eighteen forty-three to eleven seventy-seven. That's not a close margin. That's a mandate.

00:09:49.750 --> 00:10:04.630
<v Casey>So the real question underneath the horse-race framing is whether a city still working through wildfire recovery and downtown redevelopment wants a known quantity back in the chair, or something else. What does "something else" even look like right now — who else filed?

00:10:04.943 --> 00:10:16.375
<v Riley>The Tribune only names two mayoral candidates so far — Cobb, and the sitting incumbent. That's the whole field. Two names, one seat, one candidate carrying a landslide from his previous term.

00:10:17.026 --> 00:10:28.962
<v Casey>A two-person race where one candidate already has that kind of name recognition isn't much of a contest on paper. It's closer to a referendum on whether people trust the direction council's taken since he left office.

00:10:29.608 --> 00:10:49.384
<v Riley>Or it's evidence that experience matters when a city is mid-recovery from fire damage while also managing a downtown redevelopment file at the same time. Cobb knows where the old infrastructure debts are buried in that budget. That matters when the learning curve for a brand-new mayor can eat a full year of a three-year term.

00:10:49.964 --> 00:11:05.708
<v Casey>Institutional memory carries its own cost, though. A returning mayor with a built-in coalition can crowd out the debate a real contest would otherwise force. Nobody has to explain their infrastructure plan in detail if the alternative on the ballot is simply "the guy who already did this."

00:11:06.273 --> 00:11:30.281
<v Riley>That's the version of this week's underlying question that actually lands here. Every tool, every process, every returning face that makes governing easier also removes some of the friction that used to force candidates to explain themselves from scratch. Cobb's name recognition is efficient. It also means fewer voters ever hear him defend a position they haven't already accepted.

00:11:30.844 --> 00:11:40.964
<v Casey>Which is the same problem in a different outfit. Ease of decision-making for voters — pick the name you already trust — can come at the cost of the scrutiny an open race would generate.

00:11:41.637 --> 00:12:03.997
<v Riley>Compare that to the South Cariboo, where the field actually opened up rather than narrowing. David Jurek, a realtor who's run the local REMAX 100 for a decade, filed against the sitting mayor, Maureen Pinkney. He's framing his run around wanting to lead "in a different way" — more listening, bringing people together, actually getting things done, in his words.

00:12:04.562 --> 00:12:18.002
<v Casey>That's a first-time challenger against a sitting incumbent. A completely different shape of race from Williams Lake's returning-veteran scenario. One town has a known-quantity contest, the other has an actual generational contrast on the ballot.

00:12:18.652 --> 00:12:30.708
<v Riley>It raises the honest question for both towns. What should voters actually be judging candidates on — name recognition and track record, or what these councils have concretely delivered in the last term?

00:12:31.208 --> 00:12:43.584
<v Casey>Which brings us to the Canim Lake Bulk Water Station. The Cariboo Regional District confirmed this week, in its own release, that the station's opening — originally planned for this year — has slipped to spring of 2027.

00:12:44.200 --> 00:12:53.144
<v Riley>A basic water utility project pushed back a full season. That's not a scandal. But it's a data point voters in that CRD electoral area should be weighing.

00:12:53.697 --> 00:13:13.105
<v Casey>It's the data point that matters more than any campaign promise printed on a lawn sign. A council or a regional district can say whatever it likes about infrastructure priorities during an election. What actually gets built, on what timeline, tells you what the organization is capable of delivering, independent of who happens to be sitting at the table.

00:13:13.651 --> 00:13:29.147
<v Riley>CRD put the delay out in its own release, rather than the Free Press having to dig it out through a records request. That's the difference between transparency and a project quietly slipping off the public agenda for years, the way some rural capital works have before.

00:13:29.713 --> 00:13:52.801
<v Casey>One press release doesn't erase a track record of slippage on rural capital projects generally. Supply chains, contractor availability, short construction seasons — these delay everything from bridges to water stations across rural BC. The honest question for every name on every ballot this fall is whether they can say plainly which of their promises depend on things actually outside their control, and which don't.

00:13:53.434 --> 00:13:59.666
<v Riley>Hold every candidate to that standard, incumbent or challenger, in Williams Lake or in the South Cariboo.

00:14:00.166 --> 00:14:22.982
<v Casey>100 Mile House council spent time this week on traffic safety too — a fairly ordinary agenda item, speed concerns and intersection complaints raised by residents. That's a different question from the future of the rail line through town, which the province decides. This is about whether the district can act on something as basic as a dangerous intersection without waiting on any provincial process at all.

00:14:23.580 --> 00:14:42.100
<v Riley>And that's the smaller-scale version of the same test. Rail policy gets decided in Victoria. A stop sign or a reduced speed zone gets decided at the council table, this month, by people voters actually elected and can actually replace. If a council can't move on the easy file, why would anyone trust it with the hard one?

00:14:42.403 --> 00:14:58.347
<v Casey>Or read it the other way. The easy file getting attention right now is a good sign heading into an election — councils facing re-election tend to clear the visible, fixable items off their desk before voters start paying attention at the ballot box. That's not virtue. That's timing.

00:14:58.901 --> 00:15:05.853
<v Riley>Maybe both readings are true at once. It's timing, and it's also a real fix for whoever lives near that intersection.

00:15:06.353 --> 00:15:33.921
<v Casey>There's a version of the friction question sitting underneath all four of these stories, if we're honest about it. Cobb re-entering with a landslide behind him makes the choice easier for voters who already trust him. It also means fewer of them will ever sit through a debate where he has to defend specifics against a genuine challenger. Jurek entering against Pinkney forces exactly that friction in the South Cariboo — an actual debate, actual specifics, actual scrutiny neither candidate can dodge.

00:15:34.475 --> 00:15:58.259
<v Riley>Berry's argument, from the piece in today's roundup, was that some knowledge only survives because the process of acquiring it is hard. Reading a budget line by line, sitting through three years of a council term, watching a project slip and slip again — that's tedious work. It's also probably the only way most residents ever learn what their local government actually does with their money.

00:15:58.936 --> 00:16:11.376
<v Casey>So an easier race, a landslide incumbent walking back in unopposed by any serious rival, might mean fewer residents ever do that work of scrutiny at all. Nobody's forced to learn the budget if the outcome already feels decided.

00:16:11.919 --> 00:16:28.391
<v Riley>Except the flip side matters too. A city recovering from wildfire and juggling a downtown file doesn't necessarily benefit from a steep learning curve on top of everything else. Sometimes the friction that forces scrutiny isn't friction the town can afford this particular term.

00:16:29.022 --> 00:16:43.022
<v Casey>We're not going to settle that one today, and I don't think it settles cleanly at all. It depends on whether Williams Lake residents show up to ask Cobb what specifically changes from his last term in office, rather than just re-electing the name they remember.

00:16:43.522 --> 00:17:00.434
<v Riley>None of this — Cobb's return, Jurek's challenge, the water station delay, the 100 Mile traffic item — gets decided by a press release, though. It gets decided by turnout. Both Williams Lake and 100 Mile House are small enough that a few hundred votes swing the outcome entirely.

00:17:01.089 --> 00:17:21.769
<v Casey>Which is the actual leverage residents have here that they don't have on trade policy or timber supply reviews. Show up to an all-candidates forum. Ask Cobb what specifically is different this time. Ask Jurek what "bringing good people together" actually means once the budget doesn't stretch as far as the campaign speech implied. Make them answer in public, not in a mailer.

00:17:22.390 --> 00:17:31.574
<v Riley>Nobody needs a grant program or a new piece of software to do that. It just needs people in the room, asking the tedious questions Berry would have recognized.

00:17:32.232 --> 00:17:44.280
<v Casey>The room is usually half-empty at these things, though. That's the real structural gap here — not a shortage of candidates, a shortage of people willing to ask hard questions of the ones who did put their name forward.

00:17:44.933 --> 00:18:03.933
<v Riley>If there's one concrete thing worth trying before October 17th, it's this: someone in Williams Lake, or in the South Cariboo, records the all-candidates forum and posts it publicly, so the people who can't make a Tuesday-night meeting still get to hear the actual answers, not the campaign summary written afterward.

00:18:04.583 --> 00:18:21.135
<v Casey>A low-cost fix nobody needs a consultant or a budget line for. Whether anyone actually does it before the vote is the honest open question — and if you're already organizing something like that, or you've got a tip on the races we haven't covered, send it to feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a.

00:18:21.689 --> 00:18:27.265
<v Riley>We'd like to hear how it goes either way, win or lose. Hope you're having a great weekend, Casey.

00:18:27.586 --> 00:18:29.602
<v Casey>You too, Riley. Talk to everyone tomorrow.